{"ok":true,"total":3,"succeeded":3,"failed":0,"concurrency":3,"durationMs":64241,"results":[{"id":"1-silver-A","ok":true,"path":"/Users/jhuang/Desktop/JH-Projects/risk3d/_claude/themes-nightink/1-silver-A.png","sourcePath":"/Users/jhuang/.codex/generated_images/01a0e8d1-c1f7-76c1-a7db-27c75d25a3c9/exec-78b58545-8492-45cd-a511-1637d33b206a.png","revisedPrompt":"Create exactly one final raster image, a wide 16:9 premium desktop video-game screenshot for a calm world-conquest strategy board game titled “War Table,” evoking classic territory-conquest board games without copying any specific branded board.\n\nShow the whole recognizable world map: North and South America, Europe, Africa, Asia, and Australia, divided clearly into playable territories owned by four players. The visual system must be cohesive and minimalist, combining:\n• Dark “night ink” app discipline: deep indigo-navy ground, generous negative space, restrained high contrast, soft low-saturation territory ownership washes in muted coral, slate blue, pale gold, and sage. Gold is used ONLY for hairline interface accents and the single active UI element. Use one elegant high-contrast serif typeface reminiscent of Cormorant Garamond.\n• Japanese sumi-e structure: expressive coastlines in warm silver-ivory ink, drawn as tapered brush strokes that swell and thin, with subtle dry-brush texture at stroke ends. Territory colors are translucent ink washes with soft bokashi fading and gently bleeding brush edges. The indigo ground reads as indigo-dyed washi paper with only the faintest fiber texture. Add exactly 5–6 sparse, faint silver calligraphic wave strokes in the ocean. Use the zen ensō brush circle as a recurring visual motif.\n\nCritical board constraint: EXACTLY ONE mark per territory—one small, readable serif number centered inside one small brushed ensō circle. No extra pieces, tokens, icons, dots, labels, badges, or repeated marks inside territories. Keep territory boundaries readable but refined.\n\nMid-game state: one territory is selected using a brighter silver-ivory brush outline, and exactly one fluid brush-stroke arrow curves from it toward one adjacent territory. The arrow must remain elegant and sparse.\n\nBottom interface: a thin gold horizontal rule with a small brushed ensō centered on the rule. Below it, a rounded pill segmented control labeled exactly: “Place   Attack   Fortify   End turn”, with “Attack” softly illuminated as the sole active gold element. Include one small button labeled exactly “Blitz”. Keep all UI centered, spacious, understated, and readable.\n\nDo not include people or figures, military figurines, seals, stamps, fake Asian characters, decorative scenery, clutter, taglines, logos, or watermarks. Avoid photorealism and avoid glossy sci-fi styling. The result should feel like silver ink and translucent mineral-color washes on indigo washi paper: serene, disciplined, refined, premium, readable, and internally consistent. High detail, polished game UI screenshot, straight-on view, landscape 16:9.","bytes":2417944,"durationMs":55074,"attempts":1,"model":"gpt-5.6-sol","modelProvider":"openai","reasoningEffort":"low","threadId":"01a0e8d1-c1f7-76c1-a7db-27c75d25a3c9"},{"id":"1-silver-C","ok":true,"path":"/Users/jhuang/Desktop/JH-Projects/risk3d/_claude/themes-nightink/1-silver-C.png","sourcePath":"/Users/jhuang/.codex/generated_images/01a0e8d1-c251-7093-84c0-93bcfc26b44f/exec-abecfe82-ebeb-4de9-bb05-7bf84dd2970b.png","revisedPrompt":"Create exactly one final raster image: a premium, minimalist tall portrait 9:19.5 smartphone video-game screenshot for a calm world-conquest strategy board game titled “War Table,” evocative of classic territory-control board games while entirely original.\n\nCOMPOSITION: The recognizable world map—North and South America, Europe, Africa, Asia, and Australia—fills most of the screen with generous breathing room, divided clearly into playable territories owned by four players. Keep geography recognizable and gameplay-readable rather than geographically intricate. Four tiny, refined player markers sit along the top edge. At the bottom is one minimal pill-shaped phase control whose exact readable labels are: “Place   Attack   Fortify   End”. Nearby is one distinct button labeled exactly “Blitz”. One active element only; indicate it with a restrained pale-gold hairline accent. Do not add other panels, legends, logos, taglines, watermark, or clutter.\n\nVISUAL SYSTEM — “silver ink on indigo paper”: The ground is deep indigo-navy dyed washi paper, nearly flat, with only the faintest visible natural fiber texture. Coastlines and territory borders use warm silver-ivory ink as expressive Japanese sumi-e brush linework: confident tapered strokes that naturally swell and thin, with subtle dry-brush texture at stroke endings. Territory ownership appears as translucent, low-saturation ink washes in exactly four harmonious color families: muted coral, slate blue, pale gold, and sage. Use bokashi-like soft fading and gentle pigment bleed at wash edges while preserving clear borders and legibility. In the ocean, include only 5–6 sparse, elegant calligraphic wave strokes in faint silver. Use the zen ensō brush circle as the recurring UI/gameplay motif.\n\nCRITICAL TERRITORY RULE: Exactly ONE mark inside every territory—no more, no less. That single mark must be a small serif troop number centered inside a small brushed ensō circle. Do not place any other icon, dot, army, flag, label, ornament, or mark inside territories. Numbers should be small but readable and vary plausibly.\n\nTYPOGRAPHY: Use one elegant, high-contrast editorial serif throughout, visually similar to Cormorant Garamond. Restrained, carefully spaced, premium. The title “War Table” may appear once, subtly at the top, without competing with the map.\n\nMOOD AND FINISH: Cohesive, meditative, disciplined, premium game UI; dark “night ink” application palette. Gold is reserved only for hairline accents and the one active control, never broad decoration. High detail and crisp readability at smartphone scale, with ample negative space. The sumi-e map, washes, ensō territory counters, top player markers, and bottom controls must feel authored as one unified design system—not collage-like.\n\nSTRICT EXCLUSIONS: no human figures, soldiers, miniatures, weapons, seals, stamps, fake Japanese or Chinese characters, scenery, decorative landscapes, photorealism, 3D rendering, neon colors, gradients that feel digital, heavy shadows, glassmorphism, excessive UI chrome, extra buttons, extra text, watermark, tagline, or clutter. Output one polished raster screenshot only.","bytes":2425568,"durationMs":49057,"attempts":1,"model":"gpt-5.6-sol","modelProvider":"openai","reasoningEffort":"low","threadId":"01a0e8d1-c251-7093-84c0-93bcfc26b44f"},{"id":"2-kintsugi-A","ok":true,"path":"/Users/jhuang/Desktop/JH-Projects/risk3d/_claude/themes-nightink/2-kintsugi-A.png","sourcePath":"/Users/jhuang/.codex/generated_images/01a0e8d1-c3cb-7b40-b3d1-ddbd26665c65/exec-eef574a0-f9aa-4acb-9e07-cd5167d483bd.png","revisedPrompt":"Create exactly one final raster image: a polished 16:9 wide desktop video-game screenshot for a calm world-conquest strategy board game titled “War Table,” in the spirit of classic Risk. Show the whole board mid-game with a recognizable world map including the Americas, Europe, Africa, Asia, and Australia, divided into clearly readable territories owned by four players.\n\nUnify two visual traditions into one restrained system. First: a dark “night ink” application—smooth deep indigo-navy ground; low-saturation mineral territory washes in muted coral, slate blue, pale gold, and sage; elegant high-contrast serif typography reminiscent of Cormorant Garamond; generous negative space; gold reserved for hairline details and the active state. Second: Japanese sumi-e structure—coastlines made from expressive tapered soft-ivory brush strokes that swell and thin, with subtle dry-brush texture at their ends; territory fills resembling soft translucent ink washes with bokashi fading at edges; only a few sparse calligraphic wave strokes in the ocean; a zen ensō brush circle as the recurring motif.\n\nVariant direction: “indigo and kintsugi.” Borders BETWEEN territories are delicate, irregular fine-gold seams like kintsugi repair lines. Coastlines remain soft ivory tapered ink. Territory colors remain muted mineral washes. Ensō markers are brushed in gold. The result must feel quiet, precious, jewel-like, cohesive, minimalist, premium, and highly detailed.\n\nExactly ONE game-state mark in every territory: one small, readable serif number centered inside one small brushed gold ensō circle. Do not add any other markers or icons inside territories. No human figures, soldiers, pieces, flags, seals, fake Japanese or Chinese characters, decorative scenery, clutter, watermark, or taglines.\n\nSelect exactly one territory: give it a brighter gold brush outline and draw a single elegant fluid brush-stroke arrow curving from it to one neighboring territory. Keep the move readable and uncluttered.\n\nBottom interface: generous breathing room, then a thin horizontal gold rule interrupted at its exact center by one small brushed ensō. Beneath it, a restrained rounded pill segmented control labeled exactly “Place   Attack   Fortify   End turn”, with “Attack” softly illuminated as the one active UI element. Include one small separate button labeled exactly “Blitz”. Typography should be crisp and readable. Keep all UI aligned, sparse, and integrated with the board rather than looking pasted on.\n\nComposition: straight-on premium strategy-game board view, wide landscape 16:9, balanced map proportions, enough contrast for ownership colors and numbers, deep indigo negative space, subtle material depth but not photorealistic tabletop scenery. No extraneous panels, legends, resource bars, avatars, notifications, or logos beyond the modest “War Table” title if needed. Final raster image only.","bytes":2431644,"durationMs":64048,"attempts":1,"model":"gpt-5.6-sol","modelProvider":"openai","reasoningEffort":"low","threadId":"01a0e8d1-c3cb-7b40-b3d1-ddbd26665c65"}]}
